## 20. Cancellation

Opposite movements cancel.

Example:

```text
10 km east
6 km west
```

Net movement:

```text
10 - 6 = 4 km east
```

Vector form:

```text
(10,0) + (-6,0)
= (4,0)
```

## 21. Long Route Example

Input:

> “A person walks 8 km north, 5 km east, 3 km south, and 2 km west.”

Convert:

```text
N8 = (0,8)
E5 = (5,0)
S3 = (0,-3)
W2 = (-2,0)
```

Sum:

```text
x = 5 - 2 = 3
y = 8 - 3 = 5
```

Final displacement:

```text
(3,5)
```

Direction:

```text
North-East
```

Straight-line distance:

```text
√(3² + 5²)
= √34
≈ 5.83 km
```

Total path:

```text
8 + 5 + 3 + 2 = 18 km
```

## 22. Relative Turns

Suppose a person starts facing north.

```text
initial heading = North
```

Turn right:

```text
East
```

Turn right again:

```text
South
```

Turn left:

```text
East
```

This is easiest to model with angles rather than many special cases.

## 23. Turn Algorithm

Using:

```text
North = 0°
East  = 90°
South = 180°
West  = 270°
```

then:

```text
right:
heading = heading + 90°

left:
heading = heading - 90°
```

Normalize:

```text
heading = heading mod 360°
```

This works for arbitrary sequences of turns.

## 24. Example With Turns

Input:

> “A person is facing north. They turn right and walk 5 km, turn left, and walk 3 km.”

Initial:

```text
heading = North
position = (0,0)
```

Turn right:

```text
heading = East
```

Walk:

```text
position = (5,0)
```

Turn left:

```text
heading = North
```

Walk:

```text
position = (5,3)
```

Final direction from start:

```text
North-East
```

Distance:

```text
√(5² + 3²)
= √34
≈ 5.83 km
```

## 25. Movement Direction vs Heading

These are different concepts.

A person may:

```text
face west
```

while:

```text
move north
```

Therefore:

```text
heading ≠ movement direction
```

unless the statement explicitly says the person moves forward.

This distinction is critical for navigation reasoning.

## 26. Forward Movement

If an instruction says:

> “Walk forward 5 km.”

then the movement direction is the current heading.

If:

```text
heading = East
```

then:

```text
forward 5 km = (+5,0)
```

If:

```text
heading = North
```

then:

```text
forward 5 km = (0,+5)
```

This is a state-dependent operation.

## 27. Spatial Relationships

Natural language can state relationships such as:

```text
A is north of B
A is east of B
A is left of B
A is behind B
A is 5 km from B
```

Represent these as structured relations:

```text
Relation {
    subject
    relation
    reference
    distance
    frame
}
```

Example:

```text
subject = School
relation = EAST_OF
reference = Hospital
distance = unknown
```
